<Layout title="About the Festival | Breck Sky Fest" description="Breck Sky Fest is an astronomy and dark-sky festival in Breckenridge, organized by AstroTours.org. The first edition is in November 2026.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Meet Breck Sky Fest" title="Breck looks up." description="A new astronomy and dark-sky festival bringing families, visitors, students, and local people together around the sky above Breckenridge." accent="orange" icon="planet"/>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">The first edition</span><h2>A festival<br/>built on curiosity.</h2><p class="large">Breck Sky Fest is taking shape for November 2026, with astronomy at its heart and room for art, learning, and local events throughout the festival period.</p><p>The plan brings together school astronomy sessions, a community telescope night, daytime science, and a ticketed signature mountain evening. Local workshops, talks, food, and other activities can add to the program as partners join.</p><p>Exact dates, venues, and individual events are still being arranged. Final details will be posted here in October, with unconfirmed program elements clearly marked until then.</p><a class="text-link" href="/program/">See what’s taking shape <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/friends-telescope.webp" alt="A group smiling beside an AstroTours telescope at an evening program" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Good company at a previous AstroTours program.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="wrap intro-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">Why the night sky?</span><h2>There’s a lot<br/>worth looking up for.</h2></div><div><p class="large">You don’t need a science background to be curious about a planet, a distant star, or the light that reaches us from another galaxy.</p><p>A telescope gives that curiosity somewhere to go. So does an experiment, a conversation, a photograph, or a piece of art.</p><p>Dark skies are part of the festival’s focus, too. We want guests to experience the night and learn how thoughtful lighting can help protect our view of it.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/evening-telescope.webp" alt="A large telescope ready for an AstroTours evening session beside a warmly lit building" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A previous AstroTours setup. Festival venues are TBA.</figcaption></figure><div><span class="eyebrow">The people behind the telescopes</span><h2>Organized by<br/>AstroTours.org.</h2><p>Breck Sky Fest is led by astronomer Luke Huxley and AstroTours.org, which brings guided telescope experiences to communities, schools, visitors, and private groups in Colorado.</p><p>The festival builds on that hands-on approach while inviting more local people and organizations into the program.</p><div class="actions"><a href="https://www.astrotours.org/" class="button button-dark">Meet AstroTours.org <Icon name="diagonal" size={18}/></a><a href="/get-involved/" class="text-link">Get involved <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div></div></div></section>
  <Updates/>
</Layout>
